The network is fully healthy. The OSPF virtual link that the ticket describes as "collapsed" is in fact operational and has been so throughout this investigation. The virtual link runs through Area 1 (the transit area) between router r4 (router-ID 14.1.4.1, Area 0/Area 1 ABR) and router r8 (router-ID 18.1.4.1, Area 1/Area 2 ABR). Both endpoints are configured correctly — r4's frr.conf carries `area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1` and r8's frr.conf carries `area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1` — and these match the running configurations exactly, so a device restart would not lose the virtual link. FRR reports both VLINK0 interfaces as `State Point-To-Point / Full / DROther` on both sides. All five OSPF areas (0, 1, 2, 3, 4) have every expected adjacency in the Full state. The complete routing table is present on every queried router, including Area-2 prefixes (18.x.x.x, 80.x.x.x) visible in the backbone and Area-0 summaries visible inside Area 2. End-to-end data-plane connectivity between the two end-hosts (pc0 and pc12, across OSPF → EIGRP redistribution via r11/r12) shows 0% packet loss in both directions. There is no fault to repair. The premise of the ticket — that the virtual link has collapsed — is incorrect; the network is operating exactly as designed.
